te polls or expert ratings published yet", "Term-limited incumbent removes any incumbency effect", "National 2028 matchup polling favors a generic Democrat over a MAGA-branded Republican"], "rationale": "Washington's ten-point Democratic lean anchors the forecast in an open presidential race with no incumbent on the ballot. With no state polls or expert ratings available, the structural partisan lean is the strongest evidence, and national 2028 jockeying on both sides does little to change it.", "watch": "Early state-level polling, or a Republican nominee with crossover appeal among suburban Puget Sound voters, could tighten this race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Washington sits firmly in the Democratic column in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly ten points toward Democrats. No public polls of the state or expert ratings are available yet, so the forecast rests on that structural lean. The incumbent president is term-limited, removing any incumbency effect from either side.\n\nNational attention has shifted to 2028 positioning, with Democrats consolidating around early contenders and Republicans jockeying over who follows the term-limited president. A national matchup poll shows voters preferring a Democratic candidate over a MAGA-branded Republican, though that reflects the country rather than Washington specifically. Until state polling arrives, the state's lean is the best guide, and it points to a Democratic win with meaningful but not overwhelming confidence.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.873, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8189, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8232, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8205, "margin": 10